1
00:00:00,299 --> 00:00:01,460
What is a vector?

2
00:00:01,643 --> 00:00:04,350
It is a list of numbers that is also an arrow.

3
00:00:04,533 --> 00:00:07,262
Three and four, written as a column, is the same

4
00:00:07,124 --> 00:00:09,410
thing as an arrow that goes three across and four

5
00:00:09,272 --> 00:00:09,729
up.

6
00:00:09,875 --> 00:00:11,559
And what is the dot product?

7
00:00:11,773 --> 00:00:14,400
It is the one number that says how much two arrows

8
00:00:14,261 --> 00:00:14,843
agree.

9
00:00:15,034 --> 00:00:17,707
Positive when they point the same way, zero when

10
00:00:17,568 --> 00:00:20,365
they are at right angles, negative when they oppose.

11
00:00:20,557 --> 00:00:23,388
Every neuron, every embedding, every attention

12
00:00:23,250 --> 00:00:24,810
score is a dot product.

13
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,184
Let us see why.

14
00:00:27,663 --> 00:00:30,404
In eighteen forty-three, William Rowan Hamilton

15
00:00:30,265 --> 00:00:32,915
invented quaternions to multiply directions in

16
00:00:32,776 --> 00:00:33,472
space.

17
00:00:33,681 --> 00:00:36,672
A year later, Hermann Grassmann published a general

18
00:00:36,533 --> 00:00:38,592
algebra of extended quantities.

19
00:00:38,806 --> 00:00:41,104
And between eighteen eighty-one and eighteen eighty-four,

20
00:00:41,181 --> 00:00:44,036
Willard Gibbs, teaching at Yale, stripped the idea

21
00:00:43,897 --> 00:00:46,683
down to what physicists needed: the vector, with

22
00:00:46,544 --> 00:00:48,411
a dot product and a cross product.

23
00:00:48,613 --> 00:00:51,013
His notation is the one we still use.

24
00:00:51,167 --> 00:00:53,192
So a vector has three faces.

25
00:00:53,379 --> 00:00:54,677
A point in space.

26
00:00:54,891 --> 00:00:57,199
An arrow from the origin to that point.

27
00:00:57,414 --> 00:00:59,109
And a column of coordinates.

28
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:02,018
Move the point, and the arrow and the numbers move

29
00:01:01,879 --> 00:01:02,507
with it.

30
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:04,018
They are one object.

31
00:01:05,538 --> 00:01:08,347
Adding two vectors means adding their coordinates.

32
00:01:08,525 --> 00:01:11,221
Geometrically, you place the second arrow at the

33
00:01:11,082 --> 00:01:13,652
tip of the first, and the sum runs from the start

34
00:01:13,514 --> 00:01:14,244
to the end.

35
00:01:14,417 --> 00:01:17,067
Multiplying by a number stretches the arrow.

36
00:01:17,250 --> 00:01:19,820
Two times a vector is twice as long in the same

37
00:01:19,682 --> 00:01:20,502
direction.

38
00:01:20,701 --> 00:01:22,260
Minus one flips it.

39
00:01:22,458 --> 00:01:23,836
How long is an arrow?

40
00:01:24,018 --> 00:01:25,077
Pythagoras.

41
00:01:25,288 --> 00:01:28,131
The vector three, four, has length the square root

42
00:01:27,992 --> 00:01:30,450
of nine plus sixteen, which is the square root

43
00:01:30,310 --> 00:01:31,904
of twenty-five: five.

44
00:01:32,117 --> 00:01:35,063
In three dimensions the same rule applies: one,

45
00:01:35,083 --> 00:01:37,585
two, two has length the square root of one plus

46
00:01:37,447 --> 00:01:39,268
four plus four, which is three.

47
00:01:39,469 --> 00:01:41,937
And in a thousand dimensions, the rule does not

48
00:01:41,799 --> 00:01:45,040
change: square every coordinate, add them up, take

49
00:01:44,901 --> 00:01:45,824
the square root.

50
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,377
Divide a vector by its own length and you get a

51
00:01:48,239 --> 00:01:51,309
unit vector: the pure direction, with length one.

52
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:53,250
We will need that in a moment.

53
00:01:54,747 --> 00:01:56,544
Here is the first definition.

54
00:01:56,722 --> 00:01:59,416
Multiply matching coordinates, and add.

55
00:01:59,606 --> 00:02:03,255
Take a, which is three, four, and b, which is four,

56
00:02:03,299 --> 00:02:04,153
minus three.

57
00:02:04,336 --> 00:02:06,213
Three times four is twelve.

58
00:02:06,393 --> 00:02:09,009
Four times minus three is minus twelve.

59
00:02:09,189 --> 00:02:10,633
Twelve minus twelve.

60
00:02:10,814 --> 00:02:12,566
The dot product is zero.

61
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:15,628
Here is the second definition, and it looks completely

62
00:02:15,489 --> 00:02:16,207
different.

63
00:02:16,420 --> 00:02:19,343
The length of a, times the length of b, times the

64
00:02:19,205 --> 00:02:20,957
cosine of the angle between them.

65
00:02:21,148 --> 00:02:24,150
Look at the picture: a and b are at right angles.

66
00:02:24,364 --> 00:02:26,707
The cosine of ninety degrees is zero.

67
00:02:26,898 --> 00:02:29,332
So again, the dot product is zero.

68
00:02:29,542 --> 00:02:32,056
One more, where the answer is not zero.

69
00:02:32,247 --> 00:02:35,919
Take a, three, four, and c, two, one.

70
00:02:36,102 --> 00:02:39,627
Coordinates: three times two, plus four times one.

71
00:02:39,815 --> 00:02:41,056
Six plus four.

72
00:02:41,247 --> 00:02:41,817
Ten.

73
00:02:42,019 --> 00:02:44,453
Now the geometry: the lengths are five and the

74
00:02:44,315 --> 00:02:46,726
square root of five, and the angle between them

75
00:02:46,587 --> 00:02:51,715
is 26.6 degrees, whose cosine is 0.894.

76
00:02:51,781 --> 00:02:55,544
Five, times root five, times 0.894.

77
00:02:55,406 --> 00:02:56,022
Ten again.

78
00:02:56,208 --> 00:02:57,836
That is no coincidence.

79
00:02:58,049 --> 00:03:00,586
It is a theorem, and the proof is one line.

80
00:03:00,799 --> 00:03:03,052
The law of cosines says the squared length of a

81
00:03:02,913 --> 00:03:05,711
minus b equals the squared length of a, plus the

82
00:03:05,572 --> 00:03:08,586
squared length of b, minus two, times the lengths,

83
00:03:08,628 --> 00:03:09,756
times the cosine.

84
00:03:09,969 --> 00:03:12,711
Expand the left side in coordinates, cancel the

85
00:03:12,572 --> 00:03:15,756
squares, and what remains is exactly: coordinates

86
00:03:15,617 --> 00:03:18,734
multiplied and added, equals lengths times cosine.

87
00:03:18,915 --> 00:03:21,076
Two definitions, one number.

88
00:03:22,506 --> 00:03:25,497
Now the picture that makes the dot product intuitive.

89
00:03:25,681 --> 00:03:28,365
Dot a with the unit vector along b, and you get

90
00:03:28,226 --> 00:03:30,695
the length of a's shadow on b: the length of a,

91
00:03:30,715 --> 00:03:31,865
times the cosine.

92
00:03:32,078 --> 00:03:33,559
Watch the angle sweep.

93
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:36,286
When a lines up with b, the shadow is the whole

94
00:03:36,148 --> 00:03:38,196
of a, and the number is largest.

95
00:03:38,386 --> 00:03:41,104
As the angle opens, the shadow shortens.

96
00:03:41,318 --> 00:03:44,661
At ninety degrees, there is no shadow at all: zero.

97
00:03:44,852 --> 00:03:48,059
Past ninety, the shadow falls behind, and the number

98
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:48,968
turns negative.

99
00:03:49,167 --> 00:03:52,112
So the sign answers one question, same side or

100
00:03:51,974 --> 00:03:54,737
opposite side, and the size answers another, how

101
00:03:54,599 --> 00:03:56,021
much of a lies along b.

102
00:03:56,212 --> 00:03:58,658
That is the whole meaning of the dot product.

103
00:04:00,091 --> 00:04:02,752
Divide the dot product by the two lengths, and

104
00:04:02,614 --> 00:04:03,990
the lengths cancel out.

105
00:04:04,197 --> 00:04:06,915
What is left is the cosine of the angle: a number

106
00:04:06,776 --> 00:04:09,357
between minus one and one that cares only about

107
00:04:09,219 --> 00:04:09,994
direction.

108
00:04:10,177 --> 00:04:12,111
This is cosine similarity.

109
00:04:12,292 --> 00:04:15,078
In nineteen seventy-five, Gerard Salton and his

110
00:04:14,940 --> 00:04:17,351
colleagues at Cornell proposed treating a document

111
00:04:17,212 --> 00:04:20,373
as a vector: one coordinate per word, holding how

112
00:04:20,235 --> 00:04:21,521
often the word appears.

113
00:04:21,704 --> 00:04:23,797
A search query is a vector too.

114
00:04:24,008 --> 00:04:26,419
The best document is the one whose angle to the

115
00:04:26,281 --> 00:04:27,703
query is smallest.

116
00:04:27,875 --> 00:04:30,990
Here are three short documents, as word-count vectors.

117
00:04:31,205 --> 00:04:33,638
The first two share the word gradient and the word

118
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,343
loss; their cosine is 0.54.

119
00:04:36,557 --> 00:04:39,013
The third repeats chain rule and nothing else;

120
00:04:39,148 --> 00:04:41,446
its cosine with the first is only 0.

121
00:04:41,648 --> 00:04:44,343
Length does not fool it: a document that says the

122
00:04:44,205 --> 00:04:46,980
same words three times points in the same direction.

123
00:04:48,432 --> 00:04:49,286
Modern A.I.

124
00:04:49,148 --> 00:04:51,445
does the same thing with learned coordinates.

125
00:04:51,655 --> 00:04:54,361
In twenty thirteen, Mikolov and colleagues at Google

126
00:04:54,223 --> 00:04:56,838
trained vectors for words, and found that arithmetic

127
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:00,418
on them works: king, minus man, plus woman, lands

128
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:01,248
nearest to queen.

129
00:05:01,461 --> 00:05:04,020
Here is a small space where that holds exactly:

130
00:05:04,155 --> 00:05:06,338
the nearest vector by cosine is queen.

131
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,412
Inside a Transformer, every attention score is

132
00:05:09,273 --> 00:05:10,331
a dot product.

133
00:05:10,545 --> 00:05:13,695
A query vector asks; each key vector answers; the

134
00:05:13,557 --> 00:05:16,104
score is the query dot the key, divided by the

135
00:05:15,966 --> 00:05:17,445
square root of the dimension.

136
00:05:17,659 --> 00:05:20,412
High score, high agreement, more attention.

137
00:05:20,583 --> 00:05:22,336
And the humblest unit of all.

138
00:05:22,526 --> 00:05:25,347
A neuron multiplies each input by a weight and

139
00:05:25,208 --> 00:05:26,062
adds them up.

140
00:05:26,265 --> 00:05:29,051
That weighted sum is a dot product: the weights

141
00:05:28,913 --> 00:05:30,915
dot the inputs, plus a bias.

142
00:05:31,106 --> 00:05:33,904
A layer with a thousand neurons is a thousand dot

143
00:05:33,765 --> 00:05:34,540
products.

144
00:05:34,731 --> 00:05:37,756
Learning, as we saw last time, is adjusting those

145
00:05:37,617 --> 00:05:38,279
weights.

146
00:05:39,747 --> 00:05:41,930
Now the real world, in real time.

147
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:45,028
In seventeen sixty, Johann Heinrich Lambert measured

148
00:05:44,890 --> 00:05:47,358
how a matte surface reflects light, and found the

149
00:05:47,219 --> 00:05:49,949
cosine law: brightness is the dot product of the

150
00:05:49,810 --> 00:05:52,096
surface normal and the direction to the light.

151
00:05:52,287 --> 00:05:54,983
A face turned toward the light is bright; a face

152
00:05:54,844 --> 00:05:57,994
turned sideways is dim; a face turned away is dark.

153
00:05:58,167 --> 00:06:00,635
Every pixel of every three-dimensional game you

154
00:06:00,497 --> 00:06:02,998
have ever played is lit with this one dot product,

155
00:06:03,133 --> 00:06:05,942
billions of times a second, on the graphics processor.

156
00:06:06,156 --> 00:06:08,669
Here is a sphere shaded by nothing but n dot l.

157
00:06:08,531 --> 00:06:11,146
Move the light, and every pixel recomputes.

158
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:14,210
In industry, the same operation runs search and

159
00:06:14,072 --> 00:06:15,075
recommendation.

160
00:06:15,265 --> 00:06:18,415
Every product, document, or song is stored as a

161
00:06:18,276 --> 00:06:18,915
vector.

162
00:06:19,128 --> 00:06:20,915
Your query becomes a vector.

163
00:06:21,128 --> 00:06:24,154
A vector database computes the cosine against millions

164
00:06:24,015 --> 00:06:26,188
of items and returns the top few.

165
00:06:26,401 --> 00:06:30,085
Here is a catalogue of 10,000 items in 64 dimensions;

166
00:06:30,231 --> 00:06:32,734
the query vector moves, and the ranking follows

167
00:06:32,594 --> 00:06:33,051
it.

168
00:06:33,250 --> 00:06:36,332
And in physics, where it began: work is force dot

169
00:06:36,193 --> 00:06:37,127
displacement.

170
00:06:37,310 --> 00:06:39,926
Push with ten newtons along the floor, move three

171
00:06:39,788 --> 00:06:42,528
metres along and four metres up, and only the part

172
00:06:42,390 --> 00:06:45,187
of the motion along the push counts: 30 joules.

173
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:47,245
So.

174
00:06:47,431 --> 00:06:50,558
A vector is a list of numbers that is also an arrow.

175
00:06:50,772 --> 00:06:53,387
The dot product multiplies matching coordinates

176
00:06:53,249 --> 00:06:55,922
and adds them, and that equals length times length

177
00:06:55,783 --> 00:06:57,411
times the cosine of the angle.

178
00:06:57,624 --> 00:07:00,808
Its sign says same side or opposite; its size says

179
00:07:00,669 --> 00:07:01,433
how much.

180
00:07:01,625 --> 00:07:02,207
When?

181
00:07:02,420 --> 00:07:05,593
Hamilton, Grassmann and Gibbs, between eighteen

182
00:07:05,455 --> 00:07:07,298
forty-three and eighteen eighty-four.

183
00:07:07,500 --> 00:07:09,059
How is it used in A.I.?

184
00:07:09,250 --> 00:07:12,286
Every neuron, every embedding comparison, every

185
00:07:12,148 --> 00:07:13,184
attention score.

186
00:07:13,398 --> 00:07:16,343
And in real time, every lit pixel on your screen.

187
00:07:16,545 --> 00:07:20,252
Next: matrices, the machines that move whole spaces.
